@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: why a package with no source exists, and what belongs in it
' covers: internal/contract
'
' ── NO SOURCE, ON PURPOSE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
' internal/contract holds zero production lines and ~1900 test lines. It is not a
' package that forgot its implementation: it is where assertions live that belong
' to NO single package because they are about the agreement BETWEEN things.
'
' Put each of these in the package it happens to touch and it either creates an
' import cycle or, worse, gets deleted with that package's next refactor — taking
' the cross-cutting guarantee with it silently.
'
' ── WHAT QUALIFIES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' * shipped ARTEFACTS agreeing with Go constants — embedded manifests load, the
'   broker sentinel matches, model slots and defaults line up across defs and code
' * the shell library agreeing with itself under `set -euo pipefail` — the seed
'   aborts, and the bare-`exec` stderr loss that hid them
' * boundaries no compiler enforces — internal packages never importing the root,
'   the container gate speaking only canonical tier names
' * conventions about the SPECS themselves — note width, unwrappable exemptions
'
' ── WHAT DOES NOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Anything a package can assert about itself. A contract test that only exercises
' one package's own logic is a unit test in the wrong directory, and it makes that
' package look better covered than it is while being harder to find.
'
' ── WHY THESE EARN THEIR KEEP ──────────────────────────────────────────────
' Each one here has caught something. The import-boundary test caught a backend
' reaching back into the root package twice during one refactor; the seed-abort
' tests pin two `set -e` defects that each cost a night; the egress-vocabulary
' tests are the pinned half of decision D4.
title internal/contract — assertions that belong to no single package

participant "defs/ artefacts" as D <<data>>
participant "packages/lib\nshell" as SH <<data>>
participant "contract tests" as C <<app>>
participant "Go constants" as G <<data>>

D ARROW_MAIN C : manifests, model slots, defaults
SH ARROW_MAIN C : seed exit status, stderr survival
C ARROW_MAIN G : must agree
C ARROW_ERROR C : import boundaries · tier vocabulary · spec notes

note over C
  **Not a dumping ground.** If one package can
  assert it alone, it is a unit test in the wrong
  directory — and it hides how well that package
  is really covered.
end note
@enduml
